#!/usr/bin/env bash
# coding-governance pre-push: the authoritative merge bar. On a push to the default branch it runs the
# FULL gate (<prefix>/run-gates/run-gates.sh) once and BLOCKS the push if it reds. Other pushes are gated
# only by a branch bar the repository itself declares (GOV_BRANCH_GATE_CMD, see THE BRANCH BAR). Every
# refusal leaves a machine verdict in the git dir (see THE REFUSAL CHANNEL), and every push this hook
# sees, gated or not, leaves its lines in the run log (see THE RUN LOG).
# Install once per clone: git config core.hooksPath .githooks. Override: git push --no-verify.
#
# Drift note: the ACTIVE hook is the TRACKED .githooks/ dir (core.hooksPath), and check-wiring.sh
# auto-wires an unset core.hooksPath. That is NOT the same as "tracked == active by construction",
# which this note used to claim. The shared `core.hooksPath` applies unless a worktree's config.worktree sets its own,
# and the value in effect decides which hook files run: an ABSOLUTE value runs the hooks of the
# checkout it names, the relative `.githooks` check-wiring writes runs each worktree's own — so under
# an absolute value the hook that gates YOUR push comes from the checkout it names, not from the tree
# you are pushing, and a linked worktree may carry such an override. Measured at TOOL-dUnstalledConvoy-26's
# landing: the primary tree was on a contrib branch, so the push ran that branch's pre-push — no
# gate-env sourcing, no predicate 8, and the boundary's coverage check absent. Nothing wrong shipped,
# because a separate full bar had verified the pushed tree; the BOUNDARY was not the one that shipped.
# check-wiring.sh check H now compares the resolved hooks against this tree's tracked blobs and
# REPORTS a divergence as a `note` (TOOL-aWeldedTribunal-7). It reports; it does not prevent.
# (The WIRE-INTO-PROJECT.md copy-install path reintroduces the drift a second way; the same check
# covers it, since it compares whatever core.hooksPath resolves to.)
#
# COVERED DOES NOT RE-GRADE HISTORY: a push whose tip tree equals the tree a recorded green graded
# runs no leg, so a leg that reads history rather than the tree (one the manifest marks impure) is not
# graded over the pushed commits; a declared post-merge bar grades it. (TOOL-aFrugalTurnstile-2; see
# THE COVER PASS.)
#
# `pre-push --decide <tip> <remote-sha> [<remote>]` runs this hook's own decision for a push of <tip>
# over <remote-sha>, prints ONE line on stdout (`full <why>`, `scoped <base> <full-base>` or
# `covered <record>`, where <full-base> is the full green the base rests on, or `-` for an inherited
# green no scoped record may name — TOOL-aFrugalTurnstile-16; then a TAB and the vetted bar it decided for, so a caller honours it only for that bar —
# TOOL-aFrugalTurnstile-13), runs no bar and writes nothing; a push it would refuse exits 1 with an empty stdout, and a bad call
# exits 2 (TOOL-aFrugalTurnstile-7, design D9; see THE DECIDE MODE).
# WHAT --decide DOES NOT CHECK: the raw-push marker, the straggler layer and the merge-loss layer,
# which refuse a push and decide nothing, so a push it reports as scoped can still be refused by
# them; the docs-only narrowing, which it reports as plain scoped, so its caller runs a wider bar and
# never a narrower one; and anything a sourced gate-env.sh writes, which runs inside this hook in
# either mode.
#
# A POST-MERGE RED BINDS HERE where the gate-env file AT R declares `GATE_POST_MERGE` (design D8; see
# THE POST-MERGE RED): `refs/gov/bar-red` on the remote, read once with a bounded `git ls-remote`,
# forces FULL while it is an ancestor of the tip and the adopted green does not strictly descend from it.
# WHAT THE POST-MERGE READ DOES NOT CHECK: it reads the remote's red ref once, at this push, so a red
# published after the read binds the next push; it takes a red sha absent from this clone as no
# ancestor of the tip, which is wrong only in a shallow clone; and a red on exactly the adopted
# green's sha is not cleared here, since the order of two verdicts on one sha is unknown to this
# hook. `post-merge.sh` clears that case by deleting the ref on its next green.
set -u

# ---- A SIGNAL REFUSES THE PUSH — TOOL-aSignalledHook-1 --------------------------------------------
# On Git for Windows a hook that DIES BY A SIGNAL is read by git as SUCCESS, and the push lands with no
# bar. MSYS hands a native parent a signal death as exit code `n << 8`, and git takes `code & 0xff`,
# which is 0. Measured on node a (git 2.54.0.windows.1, MSYS bash 5.3.9): TERM, HUP, INT and KILL sent
# to this hook's bash, and a TerminateProcess of it, each landed a push its stub bar would have
# refused. 69e0ff0f landed that way on 2026-10-11 with its bar queued in the turnstile, and its END
# line reads `rc=0 exit=unclean`: the EXIT trap ran with `$?` still 0, then the signal killed bash.
#
# So every signal that CAN be caught is caught from here on, and ends the hook through an ordinary
# `exit` with a non-zero status, which git reads correctly on every platform. The handler ignores
# further signals first, so a second one or a write to a dead pipe cannot re-enter it; stops the bar
# when one runs, whose own TERM trap reaps its legs and drops its turnstile ticket; and refuses as
# `signalled`. It does not wait for the bar, because a wedged bar would hold the refused push open.
# The bar runs as a child this hook WAITS on (THE BAR RUNS, below), since a trapped signal is not
# handled while a foreground child runs, and the bar is the one child that runs for an hour.
#
# WHAT THIS CANNOT CATCH: KILL, and a TerminateProcess of this bash. Neither runs a handler, and both
# still read as success on Git for Windows. The lander catches them after the fact: this hook writes
# `pre-push-allow` only at its one success exit on a default-branch push, and push-main refuses to
# call a push with no such record a landing (`landed UNGATED`, no lander marker, exit 4).
PP_BAR_PID=""
write_signal_refusal() { # signal name · exit status -> the refusal line and file, then exit
  trap '' HUP INT QUIT PIPE TERM
  [ -n "$PP_BAR_PID" ] && kill -TERM "$PP_BAR_PID" 2>/dev/null
  [ -z "${PP_VIEW:-}" ] || rm -f "$PP_VIEW" 2>/dev/null
  local why="pre-push: REFUSING — this hook was sent SIG$1${PP_BAR_PID:+ while its bar ran, and the bar was stopped}, so no verdict exists for this push. Push again."
  echo "$why" >&2
  if declare -F write_refusal >/dev/null; then write_refusal signalled "$why"; fi
  RUNLOG_CLEAN=1; exit "$2"
}
trap 'write_signal_refusal HUP 129' HUP
trap 'write_signal_refusal INT 130' INT
trap 'write_signal_refusal QUIT 131' QUIT
trap 'write_signal_refusal PIPE 141' PIPE
trap 'write_signal_refusal TERM 143' TERM

# ---- S2 (TOOL-dScrubbedConduit-1): SCRUB THE ENVIRONMENT GIT INJECTS INTO THIS HOOK ---------------
# Git exports GIT_DIR whenever the repository is reached through a `.git` FILE rather than a `.git`
# directory — a LINKED WORKTREE as much as a submodule. A plain top-level clone exports nothing, which
# is why this went unseen here for so long while an adopter hit it head-on.
#
# The damage is done by any leg that creates a scratch repo: `git init` with GIT_DIR set does not make
# a repo at the cwd, it RE-INITIALISES the repo GIT_DIR names and re-derives core.bare from whether
# that path has a worktree of its own. For a worktree or submodule gitdir the answer is no, core.bare
# flips to true, and every later git call in the owning tree answers "must be run in a work tree".
# Two of this repo's own bar legs `git init` a scratch repo.
#
# GIT_EXEC_PATH is deliberately NOT scrubbed: it locates git's own helpers, and clearing it breaks git
# rather than protecting it.
unset GIT_DIR GIT_WORK_TREE GIT_INDEX_FILE GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY GIT_ALTERNATE_OBJECT_DIRECTORIES       GIT_COMMON_DIR GIT_NAMESPACE GIT_PREFIX

# ---- S5: A HOOK THAT CANNOT RESOLVE ITS REPO HAS NOT ESTABLISHED THAT THE PUSH IS SAFE -----------
# This was `|| exit 0`, and exit 0 from a pre-push hook means ALLOW. Measured end to end: with
# core.bare flipped, `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` exits 128, the early-exit fired, and a push
# LANDED ON THE REMOTE at rc=0 with the merge bar never having run. A gate that fails open is worse
# than no gate, because the green is reported.
if ! top=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null); then
  echo "pre-push: REFUSING — cannot resolve this repository's work tree, so the merge bar cannot run" >&2
  echo "  and this hook cannot establish that the push is safe. This used to exit 0 (ALLOW)." >&2
  echo "  Most likely cause: core.bare=true in the shared config, which a leg that ran 'git init'" >&2
  echo "  under an inherited GIT_DIR can set. Check:" >&2
  echo "    git config --file \"\$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/config\" --get core.bare" >&2
  echo "  Deliberate override, as always: git push --no-verify" >&2
  exit 1
fi
if ! cd "$top"; then
  echo "pre-push: REFUSING — cannot enter the work tree at '$top'." >&2
  exit 1
fi

# ---- THE DECIDE MODE — TOOL-aFrugalTurnstile-7 S4/S5/S6, design D9 ---------------------------------
# `pre-push --decide <tip> <remote-sha> [<remote>]`: the decision code below, run for a push of <tip>
# over <remote-sha>, with no bar and no write. The unattended close asks it which bar the landing push
# will owe, so the close's bar and the landing's decision are ONE computation, not a second process
# re-deriving the first. The remote is the lander's ladder, carried inline because this hook ships
# verbatim and sources no kit; given, <remote> is handed to it as GOV_REMOTE. The arguments are vetted
# here, above the journal root, so a bad call is unloggable by construction and exits 2.
# Then: every message moves to stderr and fd 3 keeps stdout for the one decision line; the run log is
# switched off and no EXIT trap stays; the refusal and vetted-bar files are emptied as names, so their
# writers skip; and the ref loop reads one synthesized line for the default branch in place of stdin.
# The three layers that refuse and decide nothing are skipped (the header's WHAT --decide DOES NOT
# CHECK); every other refusal still applies and exits 1 with nothing on fd 3.
# >>> remote_ladder_sh -- canonical copy: resolve-remote.sh in the gov lib dir (byte-identical; gated)
# resolve_remote_sh -> RR_REMOTE RR_BRANCH RR_OBSERVED RR_WHY, rc 0; rc 1 with RR_WHY on a refusal.
# The ladder, row for row, is resolve_remote.py in the gov lib dir: GOV_REMOTE, else the current
# branch remote unless it is ".", else the ONLY remote; several and none chosen, or a name that is
# no remote here, refuses naming GOV_REMOTE; no remote at all is RR_REMOTE="" and rc 0. RR_OBSERVED
# is what <remote>/HEAD names; RR_BRANCH is GOV_DEFAULT_BRANCH, else RR_OBSERVED. Fetches nothing.
# RR_GIT names the git command, so a caller holding a pinned wrapper function passes it.
resolve_remote_sh() {
  local _rr_git=${RR_GIT:-git} _rr_names _rr_n _rr_list _rr_cur _rr_where _rr_how=GOV_REMOTE _rr_head _rr_bad
  RR_REMOTE=${GOV_REMOTE:-}; RR_BRANCH=""; RR_OBSERVED=""; RR_WHY=""
  _rr_names=$("$_rr_git" remote 2>/dev/null) || _rr_names=""
  _rr_n=$(printf "%s" "$_rr_names" | grep -c . || true)
  _rr_list=$(printf "%s" "$_rr_names" | tr "\n" " ")
  [ -n "$_rr_list" ] || _rr_list=none
  # FULL refs, never `--short`: a tag or branch sharing the name makes the short form ambiguous.
  _rr_cur=$("$_rr_git" symbolic-ref --quiet HEAD 2>/dev/null) || _rr_cur=""
  case "$_rr_cur" in refs/heads/?*) _rr_cur=${_rr_cur#refs/heads/} ;; *) _rr_cur="" ;; esac
  if [ -z "$RR_REMOTE" ] && [ -n "$_rr_cur" ]; then
    RR_REMOTE=$("$_rr_git" config "branch.$_rr_cur.remote" 2>/dev/null) || RR_REMOTE=""
    _rr_how="branch.$_rr_cur.remote"
    [ "$RR_REMOTE" != . ] || RR_REMOTE=""
  fi
  if [ -z "$RR_REMOTE" ] && [ "$_rr_n" -eq 1 ]; then RR_REMOTE=$_rr_names; fi
  if [ -z "$RR_REMOTE" ] && [ "$_rr_n" -gt 1 ]; then
    _rr_where="a detached HEAD"; [ -z "$_rr_cur" ] || _rr_where="branch $_rr_cur"
    RR_WHY="cannot choose a remote: GOV_REMOTE is unset, $_rr_where has no configured remote, and this repository has $_rr_n remotes ($_rr_list). Name it: export GOV_REMOTE=<remote>."
    return 1
  fi
  # A name holding whitespace is no remote: `grep -F` would split it into several patterns.
  case "$RR_REMOTE" in *[[:space:]]*) _rr_bad=1 ;; *) _rr_bad=0 ;; esac
  if [ -n "$RR_REMOTE" ] && { [ "$_rr_bad" = 1 ] || ! printf "%s\n" "$_rr_names" | grep -qxF -- "$RR_REMOTE"; }; then
    RR_WHY="$_rr_how names $RR_REMOTE, which is no remote of this repository ($_rr_list). Name one that is: export GOV_REMOTE=<remote>."
    RR_REMOTE=""
    return 1
  fi
  if [ -n "$RR_REMOTE" ]; then
    _rr_head=$("$_rr_git" symbolic-ref --quiet "refs/remotes/$RR_REMOTE/HEAD" 2>/dev/null) || _rr_head=""
    case "$_rr_head" in "refs/remotes/$RR_REMOTE"/?*) RR_OBSERVED=${_rr_head#"refs/remotes/$RR_REMOTE"/} ;; esac
  fi
  RR_BRANCH=${GOV_DEFAULT_BRANCH:-$RR_OBSERVED}
  return 0
}
# <<< remote_ladder_sh
DECIDE=""; _dc_tip=""; _dc_r=""
if [ "${1-}" = --decide ]; then
  _dc_why=""
  if [ "$#" -lt 3 ] || [ "$#" -gt 4 ]; then
    _dc_why="it takes <tip> <remote-sha> and an optional <remote>, and was given $(( $# - 1 )) argument(s)"
  elif ! _dc_tip=$(git rev-parse -q --verify "$2^{commit}" 2>/dev/null); then
    _dc_why="'$2' names no commit"
  else
    _dc_r=$3
    case "${#_dc_r}:$_dc_r" in
      40:*[!0-9a-f]*|64:*[!0-9a-f]*) _dc_why="'$_dc_r' is not a sha of hex digits" ;;
      40:*|64:*) ;;
      *) _dc_why="'$_dc_r' is not a 40- or 64-digit sha" ;;
    esac
  fi
  if [ -z "$_dc_why" ]; then
    [ "$#" -eq 4 ] && GOV_REMOTE=$4
    if ! resolve_remote_sh; then _dc_why=$RR_WHY
    elif [ -z "$RR_REMOTE" ]; then _dc_why="this repository has no remote, so there is no push to decide"
    fi
  fi
  if [ -n "$_dc_why" ]; then
    echo "pre-push --decide: $_dc_why" >&2
    echo "usage: pre-push --decide <tip> <remote-sha> [<remote>]" >&2
    exit 2
  fi
  DECIDE=1; GOV_RUNLOG=0
  set -- "$RR_REMOTE" "$(git remote get-url --push "$RR_REMOTE" 2>/dev/null)"
  exec 3>&1 1>&2
fi

# ---- THE RUN LOG — TOOL-dLoggedFlight-4 ------------------------------------------------------------
# Every push this hook sees leaves lines in `pushes.log` under `runlog/` in the git COMMON dir, in the
# runlog kit's grammar, which that kit's README states; this block writes it and reads none of it. A
# push that reaches the ref loop writes a START after the loop and an END from the EXIT trap. The
# three default-branch refusals come BEFORE that loop, so each writes one `ev=once` line and no pair.
# The two refusals above this block write nothing: no journal root is known yet.
#
# A LINE RECORDS AN ATTEMPT, never a landing. Git fires this hook for a `--dry-run` and for a push the
# remote later rejects, and whether anything landed is git's answer. A `--no-verify` push runs no hook.
#
# THE URL IS NEVER WRITTEN, because it can carry credentials, and nor is any value that can hold one:
# a line carries `remote=<name>` or `remote_unnamed=1`, and `url_userinfo=1` where a URL held a
# `user@` part. `render_push_remote` below is the one place that decides which, and its comment states
# the rule, an `insteadOf` rewrite's typed URL included.
#
# EVIDENCE, NEVER AN INPUT. Nothing below branches on a line, and no gating decision, refusal or
# signal behaviour moved. A failed append prints ONE `pre-push: run log` line on stderr and changes
# neither the exit code nor stdout. `GOV_RUNLOG=0` in the environment writes nothing.
#
# SIGNALS ARE TRAPPED, and a signal is a REFUSAL (TOOL-aSignalledHook-1, A SIGNAL REFUSES THE PUSH,
# above). This block once argued for no trap at all, because a trapped TERM waits for a FOREGROUND
# child and the bar was one; the bar is now a child this hook waits on, so the trap fires at once.
# The untrapped version is how 69e0ff0f landed with no bar. Every exit the hook CHOOSES after the
# trap sets RUNLOG_CLEAN=1 immediately before it, the signal handler's included, and the EXIT trap
# writes `exit=clean` or `exit=unclean` from that mark: a signal now ends `exit=clean
# decision=refuse-signal`. A KILL runs no trap, so a START with no END is its signature. An unclean
# END's `rc` is whatever `$?` the trap saw, often 0: read `exit=` first. The run-log suite beside
# this file enumerates every `exit` in it.
#
# ZERO SPAWNS: builtin reads, parameter expansion, `EPOCHREALTIME` and one `printf >>` per line. A
# clone's first push pays one `mkdir` for the journal directory, and a clone that has one pays nothing.
#
# THE JOIN TO THE BAR. When this hook runs the bar it exports `GATE_RUN_ID` as `push-<digits>-<pid>`
# and writes the same value as `gate_run` on END, so the bar's own run-log line, which names its run,
# joins this push exactly. The runner removes the variable once read, so no leg inherits it.
#
# ROLLOUT. Under an absolute `core.hooksPath` naming the primary tree, a push from ANY worktree runs
# the PRIMARY tree's copy of this file: its lines appear once the primary tree has checked this change
# out, and not before. Under the relative `.githooks` each worktree runs its own copy.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT CATCH. The two refusals above; a KILL or a TerminateProcess, which push-main
# reports instead as `landed UNGATED` when git lands one; and an `exit`, or an EXIT trap of its own,
# inside the repository's `.githooks/gate-env.sh`, which is sourced below and is not this file. Every
# read in the EXIT trap is defaulted, because `set -u` holds there and a handler that aborts on an
# unset name writes nothing for exactly the push it exists to record.
RUNLOG_MAX_BYTES=2048   # the grammar's line cap, LF not counted
RUNLOG_REF_CAP=10       # ref.1 .. ref.10 on START; every ref line past them is counted into ref_more
RUNLOG_SWITCH=${GOV_RUNLOG:-}
RUNLOG_ARG1=${1-}; RUNLOG_ARG2=${2-}
RUNLOG_GITDIR=""; RUNLOG_DIR=""; RUNLOG_N=""; RUNLOG_DECISION=""; RUNLOG_GATE_RUN=""; RUNLOG_BAR=""
RUNLOG_BAR_PATH=""; RUNLOG_BAR_BLOB=""
RUNLOG_CLEAN=""; RUNLOG_WARNED=""; RUNLOG_MKDIR=""; RUNLOG_REF_MORE=0
RUNLOG_REFS=(); RUNLOG_REMOTE=()

# The two directories, with no `git` process: this tree's git dir holds the lander's marker, and the
# COMMON dir holds the journal. `.git` is a directory in a primary tree and a `gitdir:` file in a
# linked worktree, whose `commondir` names the common dir relative to it; `../` is folded so a path
# this prints is the one git would print. A primary tree has no `commondir` and its git dir IS the
# common dir. Never `.git/worktrees/<name>/runlog`, which would split one clone's journal in two.
resolve_push_dirs() { # work tree -> sets RUNLOG_GITDIR and RUNLOG_DIR; rc 1 when no git dir resolves
  local g="${1:-.}/.git" l="" c=""
  if [ -f "$g" ]; then
    { IFS= read -r l < "$g"; } 2>/dev/null || [ -n "$l" ] || return 1
    l=${l%$'\r'}
    case "$l" in "gitdir: "?*) g=${l#gitdir: } ;; *) return 1 ;; esac
    case "$g" in /*|[A-Za-z]:[/\\]*) ;; *) g="${1:-.}/$g" ;; esac
  elif [ ! -d "$g" ]; then
    return 1
  fi
  RUNLOG_GITDIR=$g
  if [ -f "$g/commondir" ]; then
    { IFS= read -r c < "$g/commondir"; } 2>/dev/null || [ -n "$c" ] || return 1
    c=${c%$'\r'}
    case "$c" in
      /*|[A-Za-z]:[/\\]*) ;;
      *) while :; do
           case "$c" in
             ..)   g=${g%/*}; c=""; break ;;
             ../*) g=${g%/*}; c=${c#../} ;;
             *)    break ;;
           esac
         done
         c="$g${c:+/$c}" ;;
    esac
  else
    c=$g
  fi
  RUNLOG_DIR="$c/runlog"
  return 0
}

# S3's fields, rendered ONCE for both lines that carry them, so the refusal line cannot carry a weaker
# guard than START. The URL never leaves this function; only two facts about it do.
#
# A NAME HOLDS NONE OF `/`, `\`, `:` OR `@`, and every URL form git takes holds one of them, the
# scp-like `user@host:path` and a drive-letter path included, save a bare directory name in the
# current directory. The shape test is what keeps a typed URL out: under an `insteadOf` rewrite $1 is
# that URL and differs from $2, which the first cut read as a name and wrote whole, userinfo and all
# (L1 of the closing diff review, round 1). The difference test stays for that bare directory name,
# which git passes as both. Builtin tests only, so the hook's exec count does not move.
#
# USERINFO IS READ OFF BOTH ARGUMENTS, since the rewrite that puts a typed URL in $1 leaves $2 a plain
# path. An authority is the part after `://` up to the next `/`, or for scp-like `user@host:path` the
# part before the colon; a plain path, a drive-letter one included, has neither and holds no userinfo.
#
# THE NAME TEST IS ITS OWN FUNCTION, outside the writer, because S1's default-branch lookup asks the
# same question and is not the writer's. It used to read the answer off RUNLOG_REMOTE, so the run-log
# suite's baseline, which stubs every `*_push_*` function, skipped S1's `git symbolic-ref` and counted
# it as an exec the writer added (gate repair at VERIFYING, TOOL-aRepatriatedFork-8 rev-4).
resolve_remote_name() { # remote name · url -> sets REMOTE_NAME to the name, or empty when the push names none
  REMOTE_NAME=""
  case "${1-}" in ""|*[/\\:@]*) return 0 ;; esac
  [ "$1" != "${2-}" ] && REMOTE_NAME=$1
  return 0
}
render_push_remote() { # remote name · url -> sets RUNLOG_REMOTE to its key/value pairs
  local a="" u n="${1-}"
  RUNLOG_REMOTE=()
  resolve_remote_name "$n" "${2-}"
  if [ -n "$REMOTE_NAME" ]; then RUNLOG_REMOTE+=(remote "$REMOTE_NAME"); else RUNLOG_REMOTE+=(remote_unnamed 1); fi
  for u in "$n" "${2-}"; do
    a=""
    case "$u" in
      *://*) a=${u#*://}; a=${a%%/*} ;;
      *:*)   a=${u%%:*}; case "$a" in */*) a="" ;; esac ;;
    esac
    case "$a" in *@*) RUNLOG_REMOTE+=(url_userinfo 1); break ;; esac
  done
  return 0
}

# THE ONE APPEND, in the grammar's own bytes. Each value is escaped backslash FIRST, then TAB, LF and
# CR, so the first pass cannot re-escape the others. A line over the cap is fitted by the runlog kit's
# REFERENCE rule, and the run-log suite compares the result with that kit's `render_line`: drop whole
# indexed fields, highest index first, counting each into its `_more`; only when none is left, cut the
# longest value outside `v n t p ev` and the `_more` counts from its end, never inside a UTF-8
# character and never inside an escape, and measure again. `LC_ALL=C` for this function alone, so
# `${#v}` counts BYTES, which is what the cap counts.
write_push_line() { # key value [key value ...] -> one line appended to the push journal
  [ "${RUNLOG_SWITCH:-}" != 0 ] || return 0
  local LC_ALL=C bs='\' tab=$'\t' line="" v i nm f size=0 best blen top cut b need
  local -a key=() val=()
  while [ "$#" -ge 2 ]; do
    v=${2//"$bs"/"$bs$bs"}; v=${v//$'\t'/'\t'}; v=${v//$'\n'/'\n'}; v=${v//$'\r'/'\r'}
    key+=("$1"); val+=("$v"); shift 2
  done
  for i in "${!key[@]}"; do size=$(( size + ${#key[i]} + ${#val[i]} + 2 )); done
  size=$(( size - 1 ))
  # STEP ONE: whole indexed fields, the highest index first, a tie going to the later field.
  while [ "$size" -gt "$RUNLOG_MAX_BYTES" ]; do
    best=-1; top=-1
    for i in "${!key[@]}"; do
      case "${key[i]}" in
        *.*) nm=${key[i]#*.}
             case "$nm" in ""|*[!0-9]*) ;; *) [ "$((10#$nm))" -ge "$top" ] && { best=$i; top=$((10#$nm)); } ;; esac ;;
      esac
    done
    [ "$best" -ge 0 ] || break
    nm="${key[best]%%.*}_more"
    size=$(( size - ${#key[best]} - ${#val[best]} - 2 ))
    key=("${key[@]:0:best}" "${key[@]:best+1}"); val=("${val[@]:0:best}" "${val[@]:best+1}")
    f=-1
    for i in "${!key[@]}"; do [ "${key[i]}" = "$nm" ] && { f=$i; break; }; done
    if [ "$f" -ge 0 ]; then
      size=$(( size - ${#val[f]} )); val[f]=$(( ${val[f]} + 1 )); size=$(( size + ${#val[f]} ))
    else
      key+=("$nm"); val+=(1); size=$(( size + ${#nm} + 3 ))
    fi
  done
  # STEP TWO: cut the longest value outside the protected keys, the first of equals, from its end.
  while [ "$size" -gt "$RUNLOG_MAX_BYTES" ]; do
    best=-1; blen=0
    for i in "${!key[@]}"; do
      case "${key[i]}" in v|n|t|p|ev|*_more) continue ;; esac
      [ "${#val[i]}" -gt "$blen" ] && { best=$i; blen=${#val[i]}; }
    done
    [ "$best" -ge 0 ] || break
    b=$(( blen - (size - RUNLOG_MAX_BYTES) )); [ "$b" -gt 0 ] || b=0
    cut=${val[best]:0:b}
    # A character cut in half is dropped whole, as the reference's decode drops it: count the
    # continuation bytes that end the value, then ask the lead byte before them how many it needed.
    b=0
    while [ "$b" -lt "${#cut}" ] && [ "$b" -lt 3 ]; do
      case "${cut:$(( ${#cut} - b - 1 )):1}" in [$'\x80'-$'\xbf']) b=$((b + 1)) ;; *) break ;; esac
    done
    if [ "$b" -lt "${#cut}" ]; then
      case "${cut:$(( ${#cut} - b - 1 )):1}" in
        [$'\xc0'-$'\xdf']) need=1 ;;
        [$'\xe0'-$'\xef']) need=2 ;;
        [$'\xf0'-$'\xf7']) need=3 ;;
        *)                 need=0 ;;
      esac
      [ "$b" -lt "$need" ] && cut=${cut:0:$(( ${#cut} - b - 1 ))}
    fi
    # ...and an escape cut in half leaves an ODD run of trailing backslashes: drop one.
    b=0
    while [ "$b" -lt "${#cut}" ] && [ "${cut:$(( ${#cut} - b - 1 )):1}" = "$bs" ]; do b=$((b + 1)); done
    [ $(( b % 2 )) -eq 1 ] && cut=${cut%?}
    size=$(( size - blen + ${#cut} )); val[best]=$cut
  done
  for i in "${!key[@]}"; do line+="${line:+$tab}${key[i]}=${val[i]}"; done
  if [ -n "$RUNLOG_DIR" ] && [ ! -d "$RUNLOG_DIR" ] && [ -z "$RUNLOG_MKDIR" ]; then
    RUNLOG_MKDIR=1; mkdir "$RUNLOG_DIR" 2>/dev/null
  fi
  [ -n "$RUNLOG_DIR" ] && { printf '%s\n' "$line" >> "$RUNLOG_DIR/pushes.log"; } 2>/dev/null && return 0
  if [ -z "$RUNLOG_WARNED" ]; then
    RUNLOG_WARNED=1
    printf 'pre-push: run log — cannot append to %s, so this push is not recorded there; its gate, its output and its exit code are unaffected\n' \
      "${RUNLOG_DIR:-(no git dir resolved)}/pushes.log" >&2
  fi
  return 0
}

# A default-branch refusal that comes before the ref loop: one unpaired line, since no START exists.
write_push_once() { # decision -> one ev=once line
  local t lander=0
  if [ -n "${EPOCHREALTIME:-}" ]; then t=${EPOCHREALTIME/,/.}; else printf -v t '%(%s)T' -1; fi
  [ -n "$RUNLOG_GITDIR" ] && [ -f "$RUNLOG_GITDIR/push-main-active" ] && lander=1
  render_push_remote "$RUNLOG_ARG1" "$RUNLOG_ARG2"
  write_push_line v 1 t "$t" p pushes ev once decision "${1:-}" "${RUNLOG_REMOTE[@]}" \
    lander "$lander" wt "${top:-}"
}

# After the ref loop: every ref line up to the count cap, the lander's marker, and the nonce END pairs on.
write_push_start() { # -> the START line, and the nonce END reads
  local t d r i=0 lander=0
  local -a f=()
  if [ -n "${EPOCHREALTIME:-}" ]; then t=${EPOCHREALTIME/,/.}; d=${t//[!0-9]/}
  else printf -v t '%(%s)T' -1; d="${t}000000"; fi
  RUNLOG_N="$$.$d"
  [ -n "$RUNLOG_GITDIR" ] && [ -f "$RUNLOG_GITDIR/push-main-active" ] && lander=1
  render_push_remote "$RUNLOG_ARG1" "$RUNLOG_ARG2"
  f=(v 1 t "$t" p pushes ev start n "$RUNLOG_N" "${RUNLOG_REMOTE[@]}" lander "$lander" wt "${top:-}")
  for r in ${RUNLOG_REFS[@]+"${RUNLOG_REFS[@]}"}; do i=$((i + 1)); f+=("ref.$i" "$r"); done
  [ "$RUNLOG_REF_MORE" -gt 0 ] && f+=(ref_more "$RUNLOG_REF_MORE")
  write_push_line "${f[@]}"
}

# Called by the EXIT trap and nothing else. EVERY read is defaulted: `set -u` holds inside a trap, and
# a trap that aborts on an unset name writes nothing for exactly the push it exists to record.
write_push_end() { # the status the EXIT trap saw -> the END line
  local rc="${1:-}" ex=unclean t
  local -a f=()
  [ "${RUNLOG_CLEAN:-}" = 1 ] && ex=clean
  if [ -n "${EPOCHREALTIME:-}" ]; then t=${EPOCHREALTIME/,/.}; else printf -v t '%(%s)T' -1; fi
  f=(v 1 t "$t" p pushes ev end n "${RUNLOG_N:-}" rc "$rc" exit "$ex" decision "${RUNLOG_DECISION:-}")
  [ -n "${RUNLOG_GATE_RUN:-}" ] && f+=(gate_run "$RUNLOG_GATE_RUN")
  # WHICH KIND OF BAR the push was gated by: `default`, `tracked` or `stub` (TOOL-aRepatriatedFork-5).
  # The class, never the command: it is what tells a stub-gated landing from a gated one, and it
  # keeps the line under the cap. Absent on every exit before the bar was vetted.
  [ -n "${RUNLOG_BAR:-}" ] && f+=(bar "$RUNLOG_BAR")
  # ...and WHICH SCRIPT, by path and blob, once one was vetted (closing review round 1 M1): the class
  # alone cannot tell the whole bar from any cheap tracked script that exits 0.
  [ -n "${RUNLOG_BAR_PATH:-}" ] && f+=(bar_path "$RUNLOG_BAR_PATH" bar_blob "${RUNLOG_BAR_BLOB:-}")
  write_push_line "${f[@]}"
}

resolve_push_dirs "$top" || { RUNLOG_GITDIR=""; RUNLOG_DIR=""; }

# ---- THE REFUSAL CHANNEL — TOOL-aRepatriatedFork-8 S2, from adopter ic's `fail_push` ------------------
# `pre-push-refusal` in this tree's git dir holds `<token><TAB><message>` for the refusal this run
# made, and nothing when it made none: it is cleared here, on every run, before anything can refuse.
# push-main.sh reads it after a failed push. It used to grep its own tee of the push output instead,
# and the bar's output is IN that stream, so a leg printing `connection` turned a red bar into an
# "unreachable remote" (TOOL-aHonedRuleset-10). A token is written by this hook and read by the
# lander; no wording anywhere can select a verdict.
#
# The tokens are adopter ic's spellings, so its reader and this one read one vocabulary. The function
# below is the one place a token is paired with its run-log decision. `gate-red` and `head-moved`
# pair with none: the bar RAN, and the decision it ran under stands. Before the ref loop no START
# exists, so the decision goes to one `ev=once` line instead. The two refusals above the journal
# root write nothing, and a lander reads that absence as its cue to probe, never as a pass.
PUSH_REFUSAL="$(git rev-parse --git-dir 2>/dev/null)/pre-push-refusal"
